The Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS),the foundational storage component of the big data ecosystem. HDFS enables 
distributed computing by partitioning large files into blocks and spreading them across a cluster of commodity hardware. It details the 
Master-Slave architecture, specifically focusing on the roles of the NameNode for metadata management and DataNodes for actual data storage. 
To ensure fault tolerance, the system utilizes a replication strategy that creates multiple copies of data blocks to prevent loss during 
hardware failures. The source further distinguishes between Hadoop 1.x and 2.x, highlighting the introduction of High Availability and Zookeeper 
to resolve single points of failure. Finally, the material outlines practical cluster configurations and the vital role of client APIs and edge nodes 
in managing data workflows.

**How does the master-slave architecture manage data distribution and fault tolerance through replication?**


